tual guidance, rather than a rule that every business must have exactly that ratio."],"sourceIssues":[{"id":"specimen-1-name","affectedPartIds":["1b-i"],"sourceQuestionPages":[77],"sourceSchemePages":[99],"affectedMarks":0,"severity":"key-label-difference","unresolved":false,"description":"The question names R. Pierre; the key''s balance sheet heading says C. Pierre.","resolution":"Use R. Pierre from the question. A change of initial does not change any accounting figure."}],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::1b-ii","maxMarks":2,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":1,"part":{"id":"1b-ii","partId":"1b-ii","label":"Current ratio","maxMarks":2,"sourceQuestionPages":[80],"sourceSchemePages":[100],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Use $64,200 current assets divided by $17,600 current liabilities.","State approximately 3.6:1; equivalent justified precision is acceptable."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"1b-ii","label":"1(b)(ii) Current ratio","kind":"table","maxMarks":2,"sourcePages":[80],"columns":["Calculation","Working","Answer"],"rows":["Current assets ÷ current liabilities"]},"checkpoints":[{"id":"s1-currentratio","label":"Current ratio before :1","expected":3.6,"responseCell":"1b-ii:0:2","sourcePages":[80],"sourceSchemePages":[100],"working":"64,200 / 17,600","tolerance":0.05,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"}],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 99–100: gross profit $174,000; net profit $83,200; gross profit percentage about 66.9%. Current assets total $64,200, liabilities $17,600 and working capital $46,600. Add non-current assets $106,800 to get net assets $153,400. Capital agrees: $76,200 + $83,200 − $6,000 = $153,400.","Current ratio = $64,200 ÷ $17,600, approximately 3.6:1. The case has enough current assets to cover short-term debts, but a relatively high ratio can indicate idle cash or weak working-capital management. The key suggests earning a return on surplus cash. Its 2:1 comparison is contextual guidance, rather than a rule that every business must have exactly that ratio."],"sourceIssues":[],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::1c","maxMarks":5,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":1,"part":{"id":"1c","partId":"1c","label":"Liquidity explanation","maxMarks":5,"sourceQuestionPages":[80],"sourceSchemePages":[100],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Explain ability to cover short-term liabilities when due.","Interpret the relatively high ratio in this supplied case as possible idle cash or inefficient working-capital use, rather than assuming it proves good management.","Suggest a relevant use for surplus cash, such as an interest-earning investment; preserve sufficient liquidity."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"1c","label":"1(c) Explain the financial position","kind":"text","maxMarks":5,"sourcePages":[80]},"checkpoints":[],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 99–100: gross profit $174,000; net profit $83,200; gross profit percentage about 66.9%. Current assets total $64,200, liabilities $17,600 and working capital $46,600. Add non-current assets $106,800 to get net assets $153,400. Capital agrees: $76,200 + $83,200 − $6,000 = $153,400.","Current ratio = $64,200 ÷ $17,600, approximately 3.6:1. The case has enough current assets to cover short-term debts, but a relatively high ratio can indicate idle cash or weak working-capital management. The key suggests earning a return on surplus cash. Its 2:1 comparison is contextual guidance, rather than a rule that every business must have exactly that ratio."],"sourceIssues":[],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::2a","maxMarks":8,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":2,"part":{"id":"2a","partId":"2a","label":"Cash book","maxMarks":8,"sourceQuestionPages":[81,82],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Record the opening cash $900 and bank $5,300 and restore petty cash by paying $245 from cash.","Show S. Smith cash $900 and discount allowed $100; P. Kan bank $760 and discount allowed $40.","Show the $400 cash-to-bank contra entry on both sides with references, drawings $200 cash, rent $500 bank and L. Lamb bank $784 with discount received $16.","Balance and bring down cash $955 and bank $5,176. Discount totals are allowed $140 and received $16; bank totals $6,460 and cash totals $1,800."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"2a","label":"2(a) Three-column cash book","kind":"table","maxMarks":8,"sourcePages":[81,82],"columns":["Line","Debit date / details","Discount allowed $","Cash debit $","Bank debit $","Credit date / details","Discount received $","Cash credit $","Bank credit $"],"rows":["Opening balances","Restore petty cash","Rent","S. Smith","Contra cash deposit","P. Kan","Drawings","L. Lamb","Column totals","Balance carried down","January 1 balance brought down"]},"checkpoints":[{"id":"s2-imprest","label":"Cash restoring imprest","expected":245,"responseCell":"2a:1:7","sourcePages":[81],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"300 − 55","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-smith-discount","label":"S. Smith discount allowed","expected":100,"responseCell":"2a:3:2","sourcePages":[81],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"1,000 − 900","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-kan-bank","label":"P. Kan cheque","expected":760,"responseCell":"2a:5:4","sourcePages":[81],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"800 × 95%","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-lamb-bank","label":"L. Lamb cheque","expected":784,"responseCell":"2a:7:8","sourcePages":[81],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"800 × 98%","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-cash","label":"Cash brought down","expected":955,"responseCell":"2a:10:3","sourcePages":[81,82],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"900 + 900 − 245 − 400 − 200","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-bank","label":"Bank brought down","expected":5176,"responseCell":"2a:10:4","sourcePages":[81,82],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"5,300 + 400 + 760 − 500 − 784","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-allowed","label":"Discount allowed total","expected":140,"responseCell":"2a:8:2","sourcePages":[81,82],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"100 + 40","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-received","label":"Discount received total","expected":16,"responseCell":"2a:8:6","sourcePages":[81,82],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"800 × 2%","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"}],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 101–102: reimburse petty cash $245 at the start. Main cash book: S. Smith pays $900 with $100 discount; P. Kan pays $760 with $40 discount. Transfer $400 from cash to bank. Rent is $500 bank, drawings $200 cash and L. Lamb receives $784 bank with $16 discount. Bring down cash $955 and bank $5,176. Discounts allowed total $140 and received total $16.","Petty cash payments total $218.15, analyzed as stationery $117.20 and cleaning $100.95. Closing petty cash is $81.85. Restore $218.15 next month to return to $300. All three available balances are debits; the bank has no overdraft in this case."],"sourceIssues":[],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::2b","maxMarks":7,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":2,"part":{"id":"2b","partId":"2b","label":"Petty cash book","maxMarks":7,"sourceQuestionPages":[81,83],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Show opening $55 plus cash reimbursement $245 to restore $300.","Allocate stationery/paper/stapler $15, $52.50, $9.95 and $39.75 to stationery; cleaning supplies $25.95 and wages $75 to cleaning.","Total payments $218.15, stationery $117.20 and cleaning $100.95; closing petty cash $81.85.","Do not enter the petty-cash expense payments again in the main cash book."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"2b","label":"2(b) Petty cash book","kind":"table","maxMarks":7,"sourcePages":[81,83],"columns":["Line","Received $","Date / details","Total paid $","Stationery $","Cleaning $"],"rows":["Opening balance","Cash reimbursement","Restored float","December 10 stationery","December 10 cleaning","December 12 stationery","December 13 paper","December 21 stapler","December 28 wages","Analysis totals","Balance carried down","January balance brought down"]},"checkpoints":[{"id":"s2-pettytotal","label":"Petty cash payments","expected":218.15,"responseCell":"2b:9:3","sourcePages":[81,83],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"15 + 25.95 + 52.50 + 9.95 + 39.75 + 75","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-stationery","label":"Stationery total","expected":117.2,"responseCell":"2b:9:4","sourcePages":[81,83],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"15 + 52.50 + 9.95 + 39.75","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-cleaning","label":"Cleaning total","expected":100.95,"responseCell":"2b:9:5","sourcePages":[81,83],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"25.95 + 75","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s2-pettybalance","label":"Petty cash carried down","expected":81.85,"responseCell":"2b:10:3","sourcePages":[81,83],"sourceSchemePages":[101],"working":"300 − 218.15","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"}],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 101–102: reimburse petty cash $245 at the start. Main cash book: S. Smith pays $900 with $100 discount; P. Kan pays $760 with $40 discount. Transfer $400 from cash to bank. Rent is $500 bank, drawings $200 cash and L. Lamb receives $784 bank with $16 discount. Bring down cash $955 and bank $5,176. Discounts allowed total $140 and received total $16.","Petty cash payments total $218.15, analyzed as stationery $117.20 and cleaning $100.95. Closing petty cash is $81.85. Restore $218.15 next month to return to $300. All three available balances are debits; the bank has no overdraft in this case."],"sourceIssues":[],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::2c","maxMarks":5,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":2,"part":{"id":"2c","partId":"2c","label":"Meaning of balances","maxMarks":5,"sourceQuestionPages":[84],"sourceSchemePages":[102],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Explain debit cash $955 and debit bank $5,176 as available current assets; the bank is not overdrawn.","Cash in hand cannot have an ordinary credit balance because physical cash cannot be spent below zero.","Explain petty cash $81.85 and next reimbursement $218.15 under the $300 imprest."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"2c","label":"2(c) Meaning of balances","kind":"text","maxMarks":5,"sourcePages":[84]},"checkpoints":[],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 101–102: reimburse petty cash $245 at the start. Main cash book: S. Smith pays $900 with $100 discount; P. Kan pays $760 with $40 discount. Transfer $400 from cash to bank. Rent is $500 bank, drawings $200 cash and L. Lamb receives $784 bank with $16 discount. Bring down cash $955 and bank $5,176. Discounts allowed total $140 and received total $16.","Petty cash payments total $218.15, analyzed as stationery $117.20 and cleaning $100.95. Closing petty cash is $81.85. Restore $218.15 next month to return to $300. All three available balances are debits; the bank has no overdraft in this case."],"sourceIssues":[],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::3a","maxMarks":11,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":3,"part":{"id":"3a","partId":"3a","label":"Manufacturing account","maxMarks":11,"sourceQuestionPages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Include carriage on raw materials in net purchases $203,000 and materials used $303,000.","Add direct factory wages $40,000 to obtain prime cost $343,000.","Factory overheads comprise repairs $5,000, electricity $2,000 and depreciation $60,000, total $67,000.","Add opening WIP $10,000 and subtract closing WIP $20,000 from cost before WIP $410,000 to obtain production cost $400,000.","Exclude office wages and finished-goods inventory from this manufacturing account; classify and present the supplied items appropriately."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key","methodCodes":null,"ownFigureRules":null,"scoringPolicy":"Reconcile the complete response, its method and presentation within the printed subpart cap. A matching number alone does not earn the whole cap."},"answerSection":{"id":"3a","label":"3(a) Manufacturing account","kind":"table","maxMarks":11,"sourcePages":[85,86],"columns":["Line","Details / working","Amount $","Subtotal $"],"rows":["Opening raw materials","Purchases","Carriage inwards","Net purchases","Materials available","Closing raw materials","Materials used","Factory wages","Prime cost","Factory repairs","Factory electricity","Equipment depreciation","Factory overheads total","Manufacturing cost before WIP","Opening WIP","Closing WIP","Production cost"]},"checkpoints":[{"id":"s3-netpurchases","label":"Net materials purchases","expected":203000,"responseCell":"3a:3:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"200,000 + 3,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-materials","label":"Materials used","expected":303000,"responseCell":"3a:6:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"150,000 + 203,000 − 50,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-prime","label":"Prime cost","expected":343000,"responseCell":"3a:8:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"303,000 + 40,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-dep","label":"Factory depreciation","expected":60000,"responseCell":"3a:11:2","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"300,000 × 20%","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-overheads","label":"Factory overheads","expected":67000,"responseCell":"3a:12:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"5,000 + 2,000 + 60,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-beforewip","label":"Manufacturing cost before WIP","expected":410000,"responseCell":"3a:13:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"343,000 + 67,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"},{"id":"s3-production","label":"Production cost","expected":400000,"responseCell":"3a:16:3","sourcePages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"working":"410,000 + 10,000 − 20,000","tolerance":0.005,"status":"official-key-checked","scoringUse":"practice-feedback-only"}],"modelSolution":["Official key, PDF pages 103–104: net purchases $203,000; materials available $353,000; materials used $303,000; prime cost $343,000. Overheads are repairs $5,000, electricity $2,000 and equipment depreciation $60,000, total $67,000. Cost before WIP is $410,000; add $10,000 opening WIP and subtract $20,000 closing WIP to get production cost $400,000. Unit cost is $400,000 ÷ 50,000 = $8. The key''s label for $410,000 is inaccurate: factory overheads themselves are $67,000.","Inventory methods are FIFO, LIFO and AVCO. Examples of payment methods are cash and cheque, with other appropriate methods accepted. Relevant accounting skills include numeracy, analysis, attention to detail, software competence, organization and communication. Give the requested number of distinct answers."],"sourceIssues":[{"id":"specimen-3a-overhead-label","affectedPartIds":["3a"],"sourceQuestionPages":[85,86],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"affectedMarks":0,"severity":"key-label-difference","unresolved":false,"description":"The key labels $410,000 total manufacturing overheads. It comprises prime cost $343,000 plus factory overheads $67,000, so it is manufacturing cost before work-in-progress adjustments.","resolution":"Use the accurate description for $410,000 and keep $67,000 as factory overheads. Do not require the mistaken label."}],"authority":"source-backed-draft","reviewRequired":true}},{"id":"poa-specimen-2-2017::3b","maxMarks":2,"sourceFormat":"poa-specimen-official-subpart-v1","sourceMarking":{"sourceId":"poa-syllabus","sourceSha256":"887519842e2bd13aca20f841303ed999f05ea962350c5c7207f63ca85c9aa55f","questionNumber":3,"part":{"id":"3b","partId":"3b","label":"Unit production cost","maxMarks":2,"sourceQuestionPages":[87],"sourceSchemePages":[103],"markAllocationSource":"official-subpart-total","criterionWeights":null,"evidenceRequirements":["Divide production cost $400,000 by 50,000 units.","State $8 per unit and show the calculation."],"evidenceDescriptionSource":"spark-unweighted-from-official-key"